titre $Key to Galerina and Phaeogalera in the Arctic - Gro Gulden
com$Gro Gulden A preliminary guide to the macromycetes in the Finse area / GF 2011

1,50$remarque
1,2 $Pleurocystidia present (often scarce), voluminous, distinctly ventricose with fusoid to cylindric necks and acute to capitate tips, apical inflation always smaller than ventral. Frb fleshy (for a Galerina) to tiny, veil forming an annulus or evanescent/absent
1,8 $Pleurocystidia absent, cheilocystidia smaller than above choice, if 50-70 µm long, then with narrow ventral inflation. Frb tiny to medium, exceptionally with a small membranous annulus (G. jaapii)
2,3 $Pileus ± fleshy and young margin incurved, ± translucently striate, veil usually well developed and forming an annulus or annulate zone, cystidia on stipe restricted to apex, stipe ± darkening to bistre from base upwards (subgen. Naucoriopsis)
2,5 $Pileus membranaceous and distinctly translucently striate to disc, margin straight, veil absent or evanescent, or at the most forming a fibrillose annulus, stipe never bistre but usually fulvous to red-brown from base (subgen. Galerina)
3,0 $Basidia 2-spored, veil remnants usually scanty (occasionally as an apical annulate zone), on herbaceous or woody remains $G. badipes* 
3,4 $Basidia 4-spored, veil remnants varying from a membranous annulus to almost nil, substrate variable
4,0 $Sp almost smooth, no loosening perispore. Typically in moist, mossy or grassy habitats and in Salix herbacea snow-beds $G. pseudomycenopsis*
4,0 $Sp distinctly rugulose with loosening perispore. Often on woody remains $G. marginata*
5,0 $Apex of stipe pruinose, lower part glabrous or ± fibrillose from veil, veil remnants often as a brim on pileus margin occasionally also ± annuliform on stipe, taste farinaceous, basidia 4-spored. Among moss in pioneer habitats on mineral soil $G. minima*
5,6 $Stipe pruinose from cystidia over entire length, veil absent, taste mild (none), basidia 2- or 4-spored. Bryophilous
6,0 $Pileocystidia absent $G. vittiformis*
6,7 $Pileocystidia present
7,0 $Pileocystidia ventricose like the hymenial cystidia, basidia 2- or 4-spored $G. atkinsoniana*
7,0 $Pileocystidia narrow, setae-like, basidia 4-spored, most spores < 10 µm long $G. perplexa
8,9 $Cystidia tibiiform
8,15 $Cystidia not tibiiform
9,0 $Basidia 2-spored $G. subclavata*
9,10 $Basidia 4-spored
10,11 $Clamps present at all septa
10,13 $Clamps absent or rare
11,0 $Spores practically smooth $G. cephalotricha*
11,12 $Spores distinctly ornamented
12,0 $In Sphagnum, stipe cystidiate over entire length, taste none, sp < 11 x 7 µm $G. tibiicystis
12,0 $Not in Sphagnum, in dryer, base rich habitats (± calciphilous), taste farinaceous, sp larger, 11.0-13.5(-15.0) x 7.0 - 9.5 µm, coarsely ornamented $G. pseudocerina*
13,0 $Clamps few (present at base of basidia and some hymenial septa, but absent in pileipellis), sp small, 8.7-10.6 x 4.5-5.8 µm, with apical pore; in Sphagnum bogs $G. stordalii*
13,14 $Clamps absent, sp larger, without pore; in moist moss mats
14,0 $Sp practically smooth, many collapsing in mounts of KOH, 9.8-12.6 x 5.3-7.3(-8.2) µm $G. arctica
14,0 $Sp distinctly verruculose, not collapsing in KOH, 12.6-15.5 x 6-8.7 µm $G. clavata*
15,16 $Sp calyptrate, distinctly so or with only tiny blisters somewhere on the surface, tawny in KOH, with distinct plage
15,19 $Sp not calyptrate, no perisporial loosening, often pale (yellow to yellow-brown) in KOH; plage absent or present
16,17 $Cystidia either narrowly lageniform or ± vesiculose, veil present, evanescent
16,18 $Cystida different, ± ventricose-capitate, veil present or absent
17,0 $Cystidia lageniform (ventricose-cylindric) and narrow, veil evanescent $G. leptocystis
17,0 $Cystidia vesiculose to pyriform, mostly apically wider than ventrally $G. hypnorum forma
18,0 $Veil absent, sp small, only rarely 10 µm long or 6 µm wide $G. fallax
18,0 $Veil present (evanescent), sp larger $G. hypnorum s. lat.*
19,20 $Basidia 2-spored, sp narrowly amygdaliform
19,21 $Basidia 4-spored
20,0 $Stipe annulate, frb ± ochre, sp rugulose-verruculose, cystidia ventricose-capitate $G. jaapii*
20,0 $Stipe not annulate, frb red-brown, sp smooth, cystidia capitate with narrow ventral inflation $G. lubrica*
21,22 $Sp truly smooth, no plage, with distinct pore or apically truncate
21,23 $Sp faintly ornamented to smooth, plage present (but sometimes visible only in profile) or absent, pore/callus present or absent, apex never truncate
22,0 $Sp ellipsoid, truncate, cystidia large, 45-60 µm long, narrowly ventricose, apically irregular and often ± capitate. In moist moss, often Sphagnum $P. stagnina*
22,0 $Sp narrowly amygdaliform, cystidia small (20-40 µm long) and lageniform. On peaty soil $P. subfusispora
23,0 $Cystidia voluminous, vesiculose to pyriform, apically often wider than ventrally $G. hypnorum forma
23,24 $Cystidia otherwise
24,25 $Stipe darker towards base
24,28 $Stipe pale, white or with shade of pileus colour, but not darkening from base
25,0 $Cystidia narrowly ventricose, with long necks, mostly rather prominently capitate $G. permixta
25,26 $Cystidia ventricose-capitate to lageniform
26,0 $Spores small, < 11 x 6 µm $G. fallax
26,27 $Spores distinctly larger, especially broader, width 6-12 µm
27,0 $On Sphagnum $G. paludosa*
27,0 $Not sphagnophilous $G. harrisonii*
28,29 $Cystidia long, without or with narrow ventral inflation, often flexuous and irregularly inflated at apex
28,30 $Cystidia lageniform to ventricose-capitate
29,0 $Sp smooth, plage absent, amygdaliform-ellipsoid, fairly large, 9.8-13.5 x 6.0-8.2 µm $G. pumila var.pumila*
29,0 $Sp faintly ornamented, plage present but indistinct (seen in profile), amygdaliform, slightly smaller, 9.8-12 x 5.8-7.5 µm $G. pumila var. subalpina*
30,0 $Cystidia mainly ventricose-capitate $G. cephalotricha*
30,31 $Cystidia mainly lageniform
31,0 $On Sphagnum $G. sphagnorum*
31,32 $Not sphagnophilous
32,0 $Pileus dull brown, lamellae dull brownish, sp with fairly distinct plage $G. mniophila*
32,0 $Pileus warm ochre-brown to yellow-brown, lamellae ochre to ochre-brown, plage visible in profile view $G. pseudomniophila*
50,0$Names marked with an * are known to occur in the Finse area /// av = average in Greenland material, frb = fruitbody, sp = spores
